In this paper, we improve the state-of-the-art for all three problems,
by introducing a suite of new techniques based on periodic graph
algorithms and complexity.
All our results pertain to polyominoes or poly(hyper)cubes and we restrict attention to ***discrete tilings***, i.e., tilings where the unit squares or hypercubes of the tiles are aligned with the integer grid.
In 2D at least, this is not restrictive: a set of polyominoes admits a tiling of the plane if and only if it admits a discrete tiling of the plane [[Ollinger09]].

## Our Results: Tiling Completion

We obtain particularly tight results for tiling completion
with a single prototile (see Section~@sec:completion):

1. Given a periodic preplacement of copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) in 2D,
   tiling completion is co-RE-complete (Theorem~@thm:periodic-completion),
   and hence undecidable.
   As a consequence, there are periodic preplacements that can be completed but only aperiodically (Corollary~@cor:aperiodic-completion).
   This undecidability result is tight by the following contrasting results:
2. Given a periodic preplacement of copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) in *1.5D*,
   tiling completion is PSPACE-complete (Theorem~@thm:periodic-completion),
   and hence decidable.
3. Given a *finite* preplacement of copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) in 2D,
   tiling completion is decidable,
   and NP-complete when the preplacement has a polynomial-size bounding box
   (Theorem~@thm:finite-completion).
4. Given a periodic preplacement of *dominoes* \D in $d$D for any $d \geq 1$,
   tiling completion can be solved in polynomial time
   (Corollary~@cor:domino-periodic-polytime).
   This is a consequence of the mathematical property that,
   if a periodic preplacement of dominoes \D in $d$D can be completed
   to a tiling, then it can be completed periodically
   with at most double the original period
   (Corollary~@cor:domino-completion-periodic).

Our results are the first to prove undecidability of tiling completion
with a *single prototile*.
The earliest undecidability result for tiling completion was by
Robinson [[Robinson1971]], who used 36 Wang tiles
(which can be implemented by 36 polyominoes [[Golomb1970]])
and required only finite preplacement.
Yang [[Yang-2013-thesis]] showed how Rule 110 can be simulated using 6 Wang tiles, and thus 6 polyominoes; together with Cook's undecidability of Rule 110 for an eventually periodic initial configuration [[cook2004universality]], this implies undecidability of tiling completion with 6 polyominoes and eventually periodic preplacement [[Demaine-Langerman-2025]], using only translations.
More recently, this bound was lowered for fixed general polygons
to 3 using finite preplacement [[Demaine-Langerman-2025]].
In addition to reducing to 1 shape and simplifying the shape to a polyomino,
we characterize the exact size of polyomino (3) and dimension (2)
required for undecidability,
while (necessarily) requiring periodic preplacement.
With finite preplacement, our results give co-RE-completeness
for two fixed polyominoes (Corollary~@cor:twopolyomino-fixed-completion).

## Our Results: Subspace Tiling

Our hardness results for tiling completion immediately apply to
the more general Problem~@prob:subspace.
In fact, all four results generalize (see Section~@sec:subspace):

1. Tiling a given periodic polyomino subset of 2D
   with copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) is co-RE-complete (Theorem~@thm:periodicsubset).
   This result is tight by the following contrasting results:
2. Tiling a given periodic polyomino subset of *1.5D*
   with copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) is PSPACE-complete (Theorem~@thm:periodicsubset).
3. Tiling a given *finite* polyomino subset of 2D
   with copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) is NP-complete (Corollary~@cor:finite-periodicsubset).
   This result was already known for both \I
   [[Beauquier-Nivat-Remila-Robson-1995,Horiyama-Ito-Nakatsuka-Suzuki-Uehara-2017]] and
   \L [[Moore-Robson-2001,Horiyama-Ito-Nakatsuka-Suzuki-Uehara-2017]],
   but we give a simpler proof based on Planar 3DM.
   %(For contrast, if the polyomino subset has no holes,
   %then tiling with \I trominoes is known to be polynomial
   %[[Kenyon-Kenyon-1992]].)
4. Tiling a given periodic polyomino subset of $d$D
   with a *domino* \D is polynomial (Corollary~@cor:domino-periodic-polytime),
   and when possible, can be done periodically
   with at most double the original period
   (Theorem~@thm:domino-periodic), for any $d \geq 1$.

The main point of comparison is a recent result of
Greenfeld and Tao [[greenfeld2025undecidability]]:
tiling a given periodic subset of $d$D space, where $d$ is part of the input,
with a single disconnected polyhypercube by translation is co-RE-complete.
Our result improves the number of dimensions from unbounded
(depending on the Turing machine being simulated) to the optimal~$2$,
improves the shape to be connected, and optimizes the complexity of the shape.
However, while Greenfeld and Tao's result is translation-only,
our result requires rotations.
For translation-only, our results need two trominoes:

: enumerate[start=5]
  \item Tiling a given periodic polyomino subset of 2D by translation
    with the two \I trominoes (\I and \Ir) is co-RE-complete (Corollary~@cor:periodicsubsettranslation).
  \item Tiling a given periodic polyomino subset of *1.5D* by translation
    with the two \I trominoes (\I and \Ir) is PSPACE-complete (Corollary~@cor:periodicsubsettranslation).

The only previous result exploiting rotations is that tiling a given
periodic polygonal subset of 2D with two polygons is co-RE-complete
[[Demaine-Langerman-2025]].
Our results improve the number of shapes by~$1$,
improve the shape to be polyomino,
and optimize the complexity of the polyomino to the optimal~$3$.

## Our Results: Tiling

With a little work (see Section~@sec:two-polyomino),
we can convert the complement of the periodic subspace
% to be tiled
into a second prototile which is a disconnected polyomino,
while expanding the tromino prototiles to constant-size connected polyominoes.
As a result, we obtain several new results for the most specific
Problem~@prob:tiling:

1. Tiling 2D with two polyomino prototiles, one of which is disconnected
   and the other of which is connected and has constant size,
   is co-RE-complete (Theorem~@thm:twopolyomino).
2. Tiling 2.5D or 3D with two connected polycube prototiles,
   one of which has constant size, is co-RE-complete (Corollary~@cor:twopolycube).
   The 2.5D result needs just two layers.
3. Tiling 1.5D with two polyomino prototiles, one of which is disconnected
   and the other of which is connected and has constant size,
   is PSPACE-hard (Theorem~@thm:twopolyomino).
   Tiling 1.5D with any collection of polyomino prototiles is decidable, and
   is in PSPACE if every prototile has a polynomial-size bounding box
   (implied e.g.\ by being connected).

Until recently, the only previous polyomino tiling results were the
original by Golomb [[Golomb1970]], which required arbitrarily many polyominoes
(depending on the Turing machine being simulated),
and Ollinger's improvement to just five polyominoes [[Ollinger09]].
If we allow general polygon shapes, three suffice for undecidability
[[Demaine-Langerman-2025]].
Very recently (and independent of our work),
Stade [[Stade2025-2tiling]] showed undecidability of tiling
with two *polyhexes* or three polyominoes,
while mentioning that it seems difficult to modify his construction
to two polyominoes.
An independent proof of the three-polyomino result was given by Kim [[kim2025undecidabilitytilingplaneset]].

%Even so, by allowing one polyomino to be disconnected or lifting to 3D,
%we improve the bound to just two polyominoes/polycubes.

Our result achieves undecidability with just two polyominoes,
%by very different techniques, and
by either allowing one polyomino to be disconnected or lifting to 2.5D.

Table~@tab:tiling summarizes the current state-of-the-art
for tiling space, allowing rotation.

