Our improvements to the state-of-the-art for tiling problems are built on
a new foundation of techniques in theoretical computer science,
differing from all past tiling results.
Specifically, our tiling results are based on new results about periodic
(infinite) graphs, developed in Sections~@sec:periodicgraphs,
@sec:periodic-reductions, and @sec:periodic-algs.
To our knowledge, our results are the first to apply results about
periodic graphs to another area (tiling), which required solving
previously unexplored aspects of periodic graphs, both algorithmic
(matching beyond 1D) and complexity-theoretic
(hardness reduction infrastructure including planar problems).

Conceptually, a $d$D periodic graph is defined by a fundamental domain,
which gets repeated by translation in $d$ directions;
see Figure~@fig:periodicgraph-example.
More precisely (and abstractly), a ***$d$D periodic graph*** is defined
by a set of ***protovertices***, where each protovertex gets repeated
for every point in the $d$D integer lattice,
and a set of ***protoedges***, which specify not only which two protovertices
to connect but also the distance vector in the integer lattice
of the actual vertices to connect (again periodically).
%We call the graph ***local*** if each edge has a distance vector of $L_1$ norm
%at most~$1$; this concept matters only if 
In most cases, we assume the graph is ***local***,
meaning that the distance vector of each protoedge has $L_1$ norm at most~$1$,
i.e., it connects protovertices in the same or neighboring lattice points.
(See Section~@sec:periodicgraphs for more precise definitions.)

Such graphs were originally studied by Orlin in the 1D case
[[Orlin1981_STOC, orlin1983matchingI, orlin1983matchingII, Orlin1984]]
and then by others in 2D and higher.
(In some cases, periodic graphs are called "dynamic graphs"
[[Iwano1987,IwanoSteiglitz1987,IwanoSteiglitz1988planarity]].)
Table~@tab:graphs summarizes the history of these results.
Notably useful for our work is that Periodic SAT is
PSPACE-complete in 1D periodic graphs [[Orlin1981_STOC]] and
undecidable in 2D periodic graphs [[Freedman1998ksat,MaratheHuntRosenkrantzStearns1998]].
We give careful proofs of these prior results
so that this paper can stand alone as
an introduction to periodic graphs.

