More interestingly, we prove the following new results about periodic graphs
and related periodic satisfiability problems:

\paragraph{Complexity Results}
\Needspace: 5\baselineskip
1. We characterize the complexity of 2D (and higher-dimensional) periodic SAT
   as ***co-RE-complete***, not just undecidable (Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat).
   Our proof of membership in co-RE uses a weak form of compactness that
   holds under standard ZF axioms (without the Axiom of Choice).
2. Many *planar* problems are hard on periodic graphs
   (PSPACE-complete in 1D and co-RE-complete in 2D):
   Planar 3SAT-3, Planar 1-in-3SAT-3, Planar 3DM, and a new problem we introduce
   called Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation;
   see Section~@sec:periodic-reductions.

\paragraph{Algorithmic Results}
\Needspace: 5\baselineskip
: enumerate[start=3]
 \item These planar hardness results are built on generalizations of classic results
   in graph drawing to periodic graphs:
   1. Every periodic graph has a periodic "nice" drawing in the plane,
      where edges do not pass through vertices except their endpoints,
      and edges cross orthogonally and only in pairs (Theorem~@thm:orthocrossing-drawing).
      This result enables planarizing any problem
      given an (orthogonal) crossover gadget.
   2. Every local periodic crossing-free drawing of a maximum-degree-4 periodic graph
      has an orthogonal crossing-free drawing (Theorem~@thm:orthodraw).
      This result allows us to draw intermediate reductions as general graphs,
      while still guaranteeing an orthogonal result
      (as needed by polyomino tiling).
 \item Contrasting with the 3SAT hardness results,
   we show that both 2SAT and Horn SAT can be solved in polynomial time on $d$D periodic graphs (Sections~@sec:2sat and~{@sec:horn}).
 \item Deciding whether a given $d$D periodic bipartite graph has a *perfect matching* can be
   solved in polynomial time (Theorem~@thm:matchingalgorithm), and in positive instances,
   there is always a periodic perfect matching with the same period (Theorem~@thm:periodicmatching).
   This problem generalizes tiling with dominoes \D
   in a periodic polycube subspace of $d$D.
   The core approach is the following:
   1. We show the existence of *finite* augmenting paths,
      at least when the graph has a perfect matching.
      This follows from a packing argument.
   2. We show further that the *shortest* augmenting path
      does not repeat a vertex, which allows us to augment the path
      while preserving periodicity of the matching.
      Effectively, we show how to simplify crossings between
      translates of the augmenting path.

%We also give careful proofs of several prior results about periodic graphs ---
%such as the basic co-RE-completeness of 3SAT in 2D and higher,
%and PSPACE-completeness in 1D --- so that this paper can stand alone as
%an introduction to periodic graphs.

