# Periodic Problems and Reductions @=sec:periodic-reductions

In this section, we give a chain of reductions to periodic problems
%that preserve periodicity and
that establish PSPACE-completeness in 1D and
co-RE-completeness in 2D and higher.

## Wang Tiling

For 1D periodic problems, our reduction chain starts from
the canonical PSPACE problem, polynomial-space Turing machine acceptance.
For 2D and higher periodic problems,
our reduction chain starts from a known co-RE-complete problem,
Wang's "domino problem".
An (unsigned) ***Wang tile*** is a square with a ***glue*** label on each edge. Given a finite set of Wang tiles, the ***domino problem*** is to decide whether there exists a tiling of the plane with these tiles such that adjacent tiles have matching glues on their touching edges.
In 1966, Berger proved Wang tiling undecidable:

: theorem [{[[Berger1966]]}] @=thm:wang
  Given a finite set of Wang tiles, it is co-RE-complete to determine
  whether they tile the plane, by translation only,
  with matching edge glues.

## Periodic CNF SAT

The ***$d$D Periodic CNF SAT*** problem is a $d$-dimensional infinite periodic version of Satisfiability.
The input consists of
1. a finite set of ***protovariables*** $v_1,\ldots,v_n$, which get copied into a countable infinity of ***variables*** $v_i^{\vec x}$ for $i \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ and $\vec x \in \mathbb Z^d$; and
2. a finite collection of ***protoclauses***, each a finite disjunction of ***literals*** (a variable or its negation), of the form
   $$ \sigma_1 v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1} \lor \sigma_2 v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2} \lor \cdots \lor \sigma_c v_{i_c}^{\vec x_c},$$
   where $\sigma_i$ is the sign of the $i$th literal, which gets copied into a countable infinity of ***clauses***
   $$ \sigma_1 v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1+\vec \Delta} \lor \sigma_2 v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2+\vec \Delta} \lor \cdots \lor \sigma_c v_{i_c}^{\vec x_c+\vec \Delta}, \forall \vec \Delta\in \mathbb Z^d.$$
As usual, the decision question is whether (infinitely many) variables can be assigned to satisfy all of the (infinitely many) clauses.
We call the instance ***local*** if all the $\vec x_i$ in a protoclause pairwise differ by at most $1$ in $L_1$ distance,
and define ***local $d$D Periodic CNF SAT*** to be the restriction of $d$D Periodic CNF SAT to local instances.

Equivalently,
a (local) periodic SAT instance can be represented by a (local) bipartite periodic graph of the same dimension: represent each protovariable $v_i$ by a protovertex, and for each clause
%
$$ \sigma_1 v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1} \lor \sigma_2 v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2} \lor \cdots \lor \sigma_c v_{i_c}^{\vec x_c},$$
%
create a protovertex $c$ to represent it, and add edges 
$(v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j}, c^{\vec 0})$ labeled $\sigma_j$ for all $j=1,\ldots,c$.

: theorem @=thm:periodic-cnf-sat
  For local Periodic CNF SAT,
  the 1D problem is PSPACE-complete and
  the 2D problem (and all higher dimensions, local or nonlocal) is co-RE-complete.

% 1D: https://coauthor.csail.mit.edu/6.5440-2023/m/PqzgxzqAMyQjfjhbf

% https://coauthor.csail.mit.edu/6.5440-2023/m/YnqSLcST7xDwpMkSh

: proof
  **2D co-RE hardness** [[Freedman1998ksat]] follows by reduction from the Wang tile problem of Theorem~@thm:wang, as in [[Freedman1998ksat]].
  For each color $i=1,\ldots,C$, create protovariables $s_i$ and $w_i$ representing whether the west and south edges of a tile are colored $i$.
  For each tile $i=1, \ldots, T$, create a protovariable $t_i$ where variable $t_i^{\vec x}$ represents whether tile $i$ is used at position $\vec x$, for each point $\vec x \in \mathbb Z^d$. As the colors of adjacent Wang tiles must match, the colors along the north and east edges of the tile at $\vec x$ are encoded in $s_{i}^{\vec x+(0,1)}$ and $w_{i}^{\vec x+(1,0)}$, respectively.
  The following protoclauses ensure each tile uses only one color along each border:
  : align*
    \neg s_{i}^{(0,0)} \lor \neg s_{j}^{(0,0)} \quad \text{for } i,j \in \{1,\ldots,C\} \text{ where } i \neq j,
    \\
    \neg w_{i}^{(0,0)} \lor \neg w_{j}^{(0,0)} \quad \text{for } i,j \in \{1,\ldots,C\} \text{ where } i \neq j.
  The following clause ensures that each position chooses at least one tile:
  $$ \bigvee_{i=1}^T t_{i}^{(0,0)}.$$
  Finally, each tile $i$ with colors $a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i$ along the south, west, north, and east edges, respectively, defines implications that ensure the colors match:
  : align*
    & \neg t_{i}^{(0,0)} \lor s_{a_i}^{(0,0)}; &
    & \neg t_{i}^{(0,0)} \lor w_{b_i}^{(0,0)}; &
    & \neg t_{i}^{(0,0)} \lor s_{c_i}^{(0,1)}; &
    & \neg t_{i}^{(0,0)} \lor w_{d_i}^{(1,0)}.
  Thus, the Periodic CNF SAT problem is satisfiable if and only if there exists a tiling of the plane with the given Wang tiles, and so the local (and thus nonlocal) 2D Periodic CNF SAT is co-RE-hard.

  **$d$D co-RE membership**: 
  We use a standard compactness argument.
  Define an order on the SAT variables by increasing distance from the origin:
  order vertex $v^{\vec x}$ by increasing $L_1$ norm $\|\vec x\|_1$,
  then lexicographically by $\vec x$, then by~$v$
  (according to a fixed order of the finite protovertex set~$V$).
  Define the potentially infinite binary tree of valid partial assignments,
  where a left branch at depth $i$ corresponds to assigning the $i$th variable to false, and a right branch corresponds to assigning it to true,
  and we omit nodes whenever the partial assignment violates a clause.
  We can construct this tree level by level, and detect whether it is infinite in co-RE.
  If the algorithm discovers that the tree is finite, then the SAT instance is unsatisfiable, and we return false.
  If the algorithm runs forever, then the tree is infinite.
  By the Weak K\"onig's Lemma (equivalent to the compactness of Cantor space $2^\omega$), an infinite binary tree contains an infinite path, which corresponds to an infinite assignment to all variables that satisfies the entire formula.
  (Unlike K\"onig's Lemma for arbitrary trees, the Weak K\"onig's Lemma is provable in ZF: for $i=1,2,\dots$, assign the $i$th variable so that the remaining rooted subtree is infinite. See, e.g., [[Bauer-2006]].)

  **1D PSPACE membership** [[Orlin1981_STOC]]:
  We give an NPSPACE algorithm for local 1D Periodic CNF SAT, which implies PSPACE membership by Savitch's Theorem [[Savitch-1970]].
  Suppose the 1D periodic CNF SAT problem is satisfiable, with variable values $v_i^z = \alpha_i^z$.
  By the Pigeonhole Principle, there are two values $x,y \in\mathbb Z$ such that $|y-x| \leq 2^{n}$ and $\alpha_i^x=\alpha_i^y$ for all $i \in \{1,\ldots,n\}$.
  By repeating the sequence of variable assignments between $x$ and $y$, we can obtain another satisfying assignment $v_i^z=\beta_i^z$ which is periodic and of period $|y-x| \leq 2^n$.

  To build an NPSPACE algorithm for satisfiability, guess the assignment of variables at position $0$: $v_i^0 = \beta_i^0$ for all $i$. Then, for each step $z=1,2,\ldots,2^{|V|}$, guess the next assignment $v_i^z = \beta_i^z$ for all $i$, check that all clauses involving variables at positions $z$ and $z-1$ are satisfied, and then forget the assignment at position $z-1$. Because the CNF SAT instance is local, all clauses will be checked by this process. If the first position's assignment $\beta_i^0$ ever gets repeated at a later $\beta_i^z$, then the instance is satisfiable.

  **1D PSPACE-hardness** [[Orlin1981_STOC]]:
  Our reduction is based on a proof by Lance Fortnow described by Scott Aaronson about time-travel computing \cite[Section~8]{Aaronson-2005}.
  Consider any language $L$ in PSPACE.
  Let $M$ be a Turing machine that accepts $L$ and runs in space $s(n)$ where $n$ is the input size and $s(n)$ is a polynomial in $n$, and assume $s(n)\geq n$ by rounding up.
  %The number of states of the machine is $|Q| \cdot s(n) \cdot \sigma^{s(n)}$
  Given an input $x$ of size $n$, we define a 1D periodic CNF SAT instance where the variables at position $i\in\mathbb Z$ represent:
  * A step counter $c$ which represents the step of the Turing machine $M$ being simulated at position $i$. Because $\text{PSPACE} \subseteq \text{EXPTIME}$, $c$ can be represented in $\gamma \, s(n)$ bits for a constant $\gamma = \gamma(M)$, or $\gamma \, s(n)$ boolean protovariables $c_0,\ldots,c_{\gamma s(n)-1}$.
  * The tape of $M$ at step $c$ using protovariables $t_{j,k}$ expressing that the symbol at position $j$ on the tape is $k$, for $j\in[1,s(n)]$.
  * The head position of the Turing machine $M$ at step $c$ using protovariables $h_j$ for the position $j\in[1,s(n)]$ on the tape.
  * The state of the Turing machine $M$ at step $c$ using protovariables $q_k$ for the state $k\in[1,|Q|]$ where $Q$ is the set of states of $M$.
  As in Garey and Johnson's proof of Cook's Theorem \cite[Theorem~2.1, p.~39]{Garey-Johnson-1979},
  we can construct a set of clauses using variables $t_j^i$, $h_j^i$, $q_k^i$, $t_j^{i+1}$, $h_j^{i+1}$, $q_k^{i+1}$ to ensure that the Turing machine $M$ simulates correctly.
  To this we add clauses expressing that the counter $c$ increments at each non-accepting step, without overflow (i.e., failing to increment $c$ when it is all $1$s).
  For any accepting state at position $i$,
  * $c_j^{i+1}$ is false (i.e., $c$ goes back to 0);
  * $t_j^{i+1}$ is set to the input instance $x$;
  * the head position $h_1^{i+1}$ is true, and $h_j^{i+1}$ is false for all $j> 1$; and
  * $q_0^{i+1}$ is true and $q_j^{i+1}$ is false for all $j> 1$.
  That is, the Turing machine at position $i+1$ is set to its initial state.
  This ensures that, if $M$ accepts input $x$, then there exists a periodic CNF SAT assignment that satisfies the clauses at all positions $i\in\mathbb Z$.
  On the other hand, any satisfying assignment of the periodic CNF SAT instance must contain a position $i$ where the Turing machine $M$ is in an accepting state because the counter $c$ cannot overflow, and the clauses ensure that the Turing machine at position $i+1$ is set to its initial state, and then the clauses simulate the Turing machine and reach another accepting state (because again the counter $c$ cannot overflow), which in turn implies that $x$ is in the language~$L$.

## Periodic 3SAT

The ***Periodic 3SAT*** problem is a special case of Periodic CNF SAT, where each clause has at most three literals.

: theorem @=thm:periodic-3sat
  Local Periodic 3SAT is co-RE-complete in 2D and PSPACE-complete in 1D.
: proof
  We use the usual reduction from CNF SAT to 3SAT [[Cook-1971]],
  but applied to local Periodic CNF SAT from Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat.
  For each clause of the SAT instance with more than three literals,
  $$ \sigma_1 v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1} \lor \sigma_2 v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2} \lor \sigma_3 v_{i_3}^{\vec x_3} \lor \cdots \lor \sigma_c v_{i_c}^{\vec x_c},$$
  create a new protovariable $z$ and replace the clause with
  $$ z^{\vec x_1} \lor \sigma_1 v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1} \lor \sigma_2 v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2},$$
  and
  $$\neg z^{\vec x_1} \lor \sigma_3 v_{i_3}^{\vec x_3} \lor \cdots \lor \sigma_c v_{i_c}^{\vec x_c}.$$
  Continue iteratively until no clause with more than three literals remains.
  The resulting instance is satisfiable if and only if the original instance is.
  If the original instance is local, then so is the modified instance.

## Periodic 3SAT-3
The ***Periodic 3SAT-3*** problem is a special case of Periodic 3SAT, where each variable occurs in at most three clauses.

: theorem @=thm:periodic-3sat-3
  Local Periodic 3SAT-3 is co-RE-complete in 2D and PSPACE-complete in 1D.
: proof
  We apply the standard cycle reduction from 3SAT to 3SAT-3 [[tovey1984simplified]],
  but reducing from local Periodic 3SAT of Theorem~@thm:periodic-3sat.
  For each variable $x$ that occurs $d$ times,
  we replace $x$ by $d$ new variables $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_d$,
  and add $d$ clauses $\neg x_i \lor x_{i+1}$
  where indices are modulo~$d$.
  These clauses are equivalent to the implication chain
  $x_1 \to x_2 \to \cdots \to x_d \to x_1$,
  which forces all $x_i$ to have the same truth value.
  Figure~@fig:3sat-3 shows the case $d=8$.
  : figure
    \centering
    $
    \vcenter: \hbox:
      \includegraphics:
        ~ scale=0.35,page=1
        - figs/3sat-3
    \quad \to \quad
    \vcenter: \hbox:
      \includegraphics:
        ~ scale=0.35,page=2
        - figs/3sat-3
    $
    \caption: Reduction from 3SAT to 3SAT-3: splitting a $d$-occurrence variable into $d$ 3-occurrence variables. (Here, $d=8$.) Circles represent variables and squares represent clauses; edge color denotes sign.
    @=fig:3sat-3

## Periodic Planar 3SAT

The ***Periodic Planar 3SAT*** problem is a special case of Periodic 3SAT,
where the periodic graph is guaranteed to be planar.

: theorem @=thm:planar-3sat
  Local Periodic Planar 3SAT is co-RE-complete in 2D and PSPACE-complete in 1D, even when restricting the grid size of the drawing to be polynomial.
  The same is true of local Periodic Planar 3SAT-3.
: proof
  We reduce from local Periodic 3SAT-3 from Theorem~@thm:periodic-3sat-3.
  Applying Theorem~@thm:orthocrossing-drawing to the graph representing the SAT instance, which has maximum degree 3, we obtain an orthogonal orthocrossing local periodic drawing of grid size $O(|V|+|E|)$.
  We view this drawing within a single $[0,1]^2$ square; because the drawing is local, this involves at most two translations of each edge.

  Decompose every edge into a sequence of horizontal and vertical unit segments, and add a variable vertex at the midpoint of each segment. 
  Then each original gridpoint is either
  1. a clause vertex with up to three incident edges;
  2. a variable vertex with up to three incident edges;
  3. a straight or bent path connecting two variable vertices; or
  4. an orthocrossing between two edges.
  In each case, we replace the gridpoint and its connections to neighboring half-grid variables with a gadget.
  In the first case, we leave the connections from clause to variables as they are.
  In the second case, we use the gadget in Figure~@fig:3sat-duplicator (or a subset for lower degree), which adds up to three duplicators ($a \to b$ and $b \to a$ to force $a=b$) to connect the variables together while forcing their value to be equal.
  In the third case, we use a subset of Figure~@fig:3sat-duplicator with only two of the incident edges (and no original variable in the center).
  In the fourth case, we use the crossover gadget 
  in Figure~@fig:3sat-crossover, which is exactly the gadget of Lichtenstein [[Lichtenstein-1982]] but drawn on a grid.
  %Figure~@fig:3sat-crossover illustrates a connection to a clause on the left and top sides, which occurs for the first crossing of a clause--variable connection, but all other crossings will involve a variable connection (like on the bottom and right sides of the figure) on both sides.
  As argued by Lichtenstein [[Lichtenstein-1982]], this gadget preserves satisfiability while removing crossings.

  : figure
    \centering
    : subfigure: \textwidth
      \centering
      \def\u{_} % workaround underscore bug
      $
      \vcenter: \hbox:
        \includegraphics:
          ~ scale=0.25,page=1
          - figs/3sat\u duplicator
      \quad \to \quad
      \vcenter: \hbox:
        \includegraphics:
          ~ scale=0.25,page=2
          - figs/3sat\u duplicator
      $
      \caption: Variable gadget. Subsets of this gadget also implement straight and turn gridpoints.
      @=fig:3sat-duplicator
    
    \medskip
    
    % https://courses.csail.mit.edu/6.5440/fall23/lectures/L05.html
    : subfigure: \textwidth
      \centering
      \def\u{_} % workaround underscore bug
      $
      \vcenter: \hbox:
        \includegraphics:
          ~ scale=0.2,page=1
          - figs/3sat\u crossover
      \quad \to \quad
      \vcenter: \hbox:
        \includegraphics:
          ~ scale=0.35,page=2
          - figs/3sat\u crossover
      $
      \caption: Crossover gadget, based on \cite[Figs.~4 and 5]{Lichtenstein-1982}.
      @=fig:3sat-crossover
    \caption: Reduction from 3SAT-3 to Planar 3SAT.

  As shown by the figures, the gadgets can be drawn in a grid of constant size, so the grid size of the resulting drawing is $O(|V|+|E|)$.
  (Specifically, Figure~@fig:3sat-duplicator refines by a factor of $4$, and Figure~@fig:3sat-crossover refines by a factor of $10$, so we use the least common multiple of~$20$.)

  The gadgets of Figures~@fig:3sat-duplicator and @fig:3sat-crossover
  introduce variable vertices of degree larger than~$3$.
  We can reduce to Periodic Planar 3SAT-3 by using
  the cycle construction of Theorem~@thm:periodic-3sat-3,
  while taking care to use a constant factor of additional grid size.
  Specifically, all variable vertices have incident edges at angles that are integer multiples of $45^\circ$, and Figure~@fig:3sat-3 shows how to solve this case while refining the grid by a factor of~$4$.

## Periodic Planar 1-in-3SAT

The ***Periodic Planar 1-in-3SAT*** problem is a modification of Periodic Planar 3SAT, where for each clause, exactly one literal must be true.

: theorem @=thm:planar-1-in-3sat
  Local Periodic Planar 1-in-3SAT is co-RE-complete in 2D and PSPACE-complete in 1D, even when restricting the grid size of the drawing to be polynomial.
  The same is true of local Periodic Planar 1-in-3SAT-3.
: proof
  Membership follows by expressing each 1-in-3 clause as four CNF clauses and applying Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat.
  For hardness, we follow the proof of Dyer and Frieze [[Dyer-Frieze-1986]] that 1-in-3SAT is NP-hard, but reduce from local Periodic Planar 3SAT-3 of Theorem~@thm:planar-3sat.
  Given a local Periodic Planar 3SAT-3 instance, we replace each clause with the gadget of Figure~@fig:3sat-to-1in3sat, which ensures that exactly one of the three literals is true.
  Each occurrence of an original variable is replaced by one occurrence, while each new variable occurs at most twice, so every variable still occurs at most three times.
  The gadget is drawn on a grid of constant size, so the grid size of the resulting drawing is only a constant factor larger.

: figure
  \centering
  \def\u{_} % workaround underscore bug
  $
  \vcenter: \hbox:
    \includegraphics:
      ~ scale=0.4,page=1
      - figs/3sat\u to\u 1in3sat
  \quad \to \quad
  \vcenter: \hbox:
    \includegraphics:
      ~ scale=0.4,page=2
      - figs/3sat\u to\u 1in3sat
  $
  \caption: Reduction from 3SAT to 1-in-3SAT.
    Based on \cite[Figure 1]{Dyer-Frieze-1986}.
  @=fig:3sat-to-1in3sat

%This version allows negations.  We could probably avoid them using other proofs.
% https://courses.csail.mit.edu/6.5440/fall23/lectures/L05.html?notes=4&images=8
% Laroche 1993 / Mulzer & Rote 2008

## Periodic Planar 3DM

In ***3DM (3-Dimensional Matching)***, we are given three disjoint sets $R,G,B$ (representing colors Red, Green, and Blue) and a set $T \subseteq R \times G \times B$ of trichromatic triples. A (perfect) ***3D matching*** is a subset $M \subseteq T$ of trichromatic triples that cover every element $x \in R \cup G \cup B$ exactly once, i.e., exactly one $m \in M$ contains $x$.

A 3DM instance can be represented by a bipartite graph, where the elements of $R$, $G$, and $B$ are vertices colored red, green, and blue; and every trichromatic triple $t=(r,g,b)\in T$ is a blank vertex $t$ with an edge to vertices $r$, $g$, and $b$. The problem is then to determine whether a subset $M \subseteq T$ of the blank vertices can be selected such that each colored vertex has exactly one blank neighbor in the set.

The (local) ***Periodic 3DM*** problem is the generalization of 3DM to periodic input bipartite graphs, and ***Periodic Planar 3DM*** further restricts the periodic bipartite graph to be planar.

: theorem @=thm:planar-3dm
  Local Periodic Planar 3DM is co-RE-complete in 2D and PSPACE-complete in 1D, even when every colored vertex has degree 2 or 3.
: proof
  Membership follows by encoding the choice of triples as a local Periodic CNF SAT instance and applying Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat.
  We follow Dyer and Frieze's NP-hardness reduction
  from (finite) Planar 1-in-3SAT to (finite) Planar 3DM [[Dyer-Frieze-1986]].
  Figure~@fig:3dm-gadgets gives the gadgets that locally replace each variable and clause, which are drawn on a constant-size grid.
  In the resulting instances, every element belongs to two or three triples.

: figure
  \centering
  % https://courses.csail.mit.edu/6.5440/fall23/lectures/L05.html?notes=4&images=14
  % https://cocreate.csail.mit.edu/r/n9HhBP2aHZvaWhvFP#F6fN9XoLPBQHK54mQ
  \subcaptionbox:
    - Variable gadget.
    - \includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figs/3DM-var}
  \hfil
  \subcaptionbox:
    - Clause gadget.
    - \includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figs/3DM-clause}
  \caption:
    Gadgets from Dyer and Frieze's reduction from Planar 1-in-3SAT to Planar 3DM.  Based on \cite[Figures 3, 4, and 5]{Dyer-Frieze-1986}.
  @=fig:3dm-gadgets

%maybe we should just reduce from 1-in-E3SAT-E3
% https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19204804/

## Periodic Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation

In ***Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation***, we are given a 3-regular undirected graph with vertex set $V = T \cup Z$, where edges are colored red, green, or blue.
Vertices in $T$ have exactly one incident edge of each color (trichromatic), and vertices in $Z$ are incident to edges of exactly one color (monochromatic).
The goal of the problem is to orient (direct) the edges so that the vertices in $T$ have indegree 0 or 3, and vertices in $Z$ have indegree exactly~1.
Thus we call vertices in $T$ ***0-or-3-in-3*** and vertices in $Z$ ***1-in-3***.
This problem is a restricted version of the 1-in-3 Graph Orientation problem of Horiyama et al.~[[Horiyama-Ito-Nakatsuka-Suzuki-Uehara-2017]]
and the planar version in [[planar-graph-orientation]].
(Our trichromatic constraints are new.)

The ***Periodic Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation*** problem is one for which the underlying graph is infinite, periodic, and planar.

: theorem @=thm:planar-graph-orientation
  Local Periodic Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation is co-RE-complete in 2D and PSPACE-complete in 1D.
  Finite Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation is NP-complete.

: proof
  Membership follows by encoding the orientation constraints as a local Periodic CNF SAT instance and applying Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat.
  We reduce from local Periodic Planar 3DM of Theorem~@thm:planar-3dm,
  or for the finite case, from finite Planar 3DM [[Dyer-Frieze-1986]].
  Given a planar drawing of a planar [periodic] 3DM instance, we transform it into a planar [periodic] trichromatic graph instance.
  First take the same graph and drawing, but forget the vertex colors.
  Vertices in $T$ in the 3DM instance become the vertices in $T$ in the trichromatic graph orientation instance, and vertices in $Z$ are the vertices in $R \cup G \cup B$ in the 3DM instance.
  Edges incident to $R$ in 3DM are colored red, edges incident to $G$ are colored green, and edges incident to $B$ are colored blue.
  Because all edges connect a colored vertex to a blank vertex in 3DM, the edge colors are well-defined in the trichromatic graph orientation instance.

  It remains to make the graph 3-regular.
  Every vertex in $T$ has degree 3, and by Theorem~@thm:planar-3dm,
  every vertex in $Z$ has degree 2 or~3.
  Contract every degree-2 vertex $v$ in $Z$ into one of its neighbors, concatenating its two incident edges in the drawing.
  The two edges have the same color because $v$ is monochromatic,
  so we can assign this color to the contracted edge and
  every vertex in $T$ remains incident to one edge of each color.
  An orientation of the contracted edge corresponds to a unique orientation of the original two-edge path in which one edge enters $v$ and the other leaves~$v$, so this transformation preserves solutions.
  Contraction preserves planarity and makes the graph at most 2-local; expanding the prototile by a factor of 2 makes it local again.

  Given a perfect 3D matching $M$, we can solve trichromatic graph orientation
  by orienting the three edges incident to each $t\in M$ away from it, orienting the edges incident to each $t\in T\setminus M$ towards it,
  and then performing the contractions described above.
  Every vertex in $T$ has indegree 0 or 3 by construction,
  and every colored vertex has indegree 1 because it is covered by exactly one triple in~$M$;
  contraction preserves these properties.

  Conversely, given a solution to the trichromatic graph orientation instance, we can extract a perfect matching for the 3DM instance as follows:
  split every contracted edge by restoring its degree-2 vertex in $Z$, and put in $M$ all vertices $t \in T$ of indegree~$0$.
  The indegree-$1$ condition for vertices in $Z$ ensures that every colored vertex is covered exactly once, so these vertices form a perfect matching $M$ for the 3DM instance.

# Periodic Graph Algorithms @=sec:periodic-algs

In this section, we give polynomial-time algorithms
for some $d$D periodic graph problems:
tractable cases of SAT
(Sections~@sec:2sat and~@sec:horn)
and bipartite perfect matching
(Section~@sec:matching).

## 2SAT and Reachability @=sec:2sat

The ***Periodic 2SAT*** problem is the special case of Periodic CNF SAT where each clause has at most two literals.  The 1-dimensional version of this problem was shown to be solvable in polynomial time by [[MaratheHuntStearnsRosenkrantz1995]], while the 2-dimensional version was shown to be decidable by [[Freedman1998ksat]].

Here we observe that existing results [[Freedman1998ksat, HoftingWanke1993]]
imply that $d$-dimensional local Periodic 2SAT can be solved in polynomial time:

: theorem @=thm:periodic-2sat
  Local Periodic 2SAT can be solved in polynomial time in $d$D.
: proof
  Freedman [[Freedman1998ksat]] reduces this problem to the ***Periodic Reachability*** problem: given a directed periodic graph $\PER{G} = (\PER{V}, \PER{E})$ and two vertices $u, v \in \PER{V}$,
  determine whether there exists a path from $u$ to $v$.
  %
  Their reduction is as follows.
  Given a $d$-dimensional local Periodic 2SAT instance, construct a directed periodic graph which has a vertex for each literal, and edges $\overline{x} \to y$ and $\overline{y} \to x$ for each width-2 clause $x \lor y$, where $\overline{x}$ denotes the negation of the literal $x$.
  Treat each width-1 clause $x$ as the clause $x \lor x$, which adds the edge $\overline{x} \to x$.
  Then a solution to the $d$-dimensional Periodic 2SAT instance exists if and only if there is no variable $x$ with both a path from $x$ to $\overline{x}$ and a path from $\overline{x}$ to $x$.
  Since the instance is periodic, this condition needs only to be checked once for each protovariable.

  Hofting and Wanke [[HoftingWanke1993]] give a polynomial-time algorithm for Periodic Reachability in $d$-dimensional directed local periodic graphs.
  It follows that $d$-dimensional local Periodic 2SAT can be solved in polynomial time as well.

## (Dual) Horn SAT @=sec:horn

The ***Periodic Horn SAT*** problem is the special case of Periodic CNF SAT where every clause has at most one positive literal.
The ***Periodic Dual Horn SAT*** problem is the opposite case where every clause has at most one negative literal.
Marathe et al.~[[MaratheHuntRosenkrantzStearns1998]] gave a polynomial-time algorithm for the 1D and 2D versions of these problems.
Here we observe that this algorithm works independent of the number of dimensions and can be made to run in linear time.

: theorem @=thm:periodic-horn
  Periodic Horn SAT and Periodic Dual Horn SAT
  can be solved in linear time in $d$D.
  Furthermore, all satisfiable instances have periodic solutions with period 1.

: proof
  Given an instance of Periodic Horn SAT, we can construct a finite Horn SAT instance by identifying all the variables corresponding to the same protovariable.
  A solution to this Horn SAT instance is equivalent to a 1-periodic solution to the original Periodic Horn SAT problem.
  Because Horn SAT can be solved in linear time [[DowlingGallier1984]], it remains only to show that a solution to the Periodic Horn SAT instance exists if and only if a 1-periodic solution exists.

  A Horn clause with a positive literal, such as $x \lor \neg y \lor \neg z$, can be viewed as an inference rule
  "Given $y$ and $z$, derive $x$".
  (In the base case, a positive unit clause $x$ gives the inference rule "derive $x$".)
  Similarly, a Horn clause with only negative literals, such as $\neg y \lor \neg z$, can be viewed as the rule "Given $y$ and $z$, derive $\bot$".
  Call a variable ***derivable*** if it can be obtained by finitely many applications of these rules.
  A solution to a (possibly infinite) Horn SAT instance exists if and only if $\bot$ is not derivable,
  and one such solution is obtained by setting the derivable variables to true and all other variables to false.
  For Periodic Horn SAT, if a variable $v_i^{\vec x}$ is derivable, then so are all variables $v_i^{\vec y}$ corresponding to the same protovariable $v_i$;
  in other words, the set of derivable variables is 1-periodic.
  To see this, take the derivation of $v_i^{\vec x}$ and translate it by $\vec \Delta = \vec y - \vec x$;
  because the instance contains every translate of every Horn rule, this translated sequence is again a valid derivation, of $v_i^{\vec x+\vec\Delta} = v_i^{\vec y}$.
  Thus a solution to a Periodic Horn SAT instance exists if and only if a 1-periodic solution exists.

  The same applies to Periodic Dual Horn SAT by negation of all variables.

## Periodic Perfect Matching @=sec:matching
A ***matching*** in an undirected graph $G=(V,E)$ is a set $M\subseteq E$ of edges that are disjoint. A matching is ***perfect*** if every vertex of $V$ belongs to an edge in $M$.
For a $d$-dimensional periodic graph $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$, a matching $M\subseteq\PER{E}$ is ***periodic*** if there are $d$ independent vectors $\vec \Delta_1,\ldots, \vec \Delta_d \in\mathbb Z^d$ such that an edge $(u^{\vec x},v^{\vec y})\in M$ if and only if $(u^{\vec x+\vec\Delta_i},v^{\vec y+\vec\Delta_i})\in M$ for all $i \in \{1,\ldots,d\}$, or equivalently, if the subgraph $\PER{G}_M = (\PER{V},M)$ is a periodic graph (possibly with a different period than $\PER{G}$). If the period of $\PER{G}$ and $\PER{G}_M$ is the same, i.e., $\vec\Delta_i$ is the unit vector along the $i$th axis, then we say the matching is ***1-periodic***.

For a (non-perfect) matching $M$, a vertex of $\PER{V}$ is ***free*** if it is not in any edge of $M$. An ***alternating walk*** is a walk in $\PER{G}$ alternating between edges not in $M$ and edges in $M$. In the discussion below, we allow alternating walks to be non-simple, i.e., to visit edges and vertices several times. When the walk is simple, we call it an ***alternating path***.
If an alternating path $P$ joins two free vertices, then the matching $M$ can be augmented by taking the symmetric difference $M\oplus P$ between $M$ and $P$. In that case, $P$ is called an ***augmenting path***.
Note that two alternating walks $P$ and $Q$ can never properly intersect: If they meet at some internal vertex $v$, then because exactly one of the edges incident to $v$ is in $M$, that edge must be in both $P$ and $Q$ and so $P$ and $Q$ share some subpath containing $v$.
The ***diameter*** of a path or walk is the smallest $D$ such that for all $u^{\vec x}$ and $v^{\vec y}$ in $P$, $\|\vec x - \vec y\|_\infty \leq D$.

: lemma @=lem:shortaugmenting
  Let $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$ be a $d$-dimensional local periodic graph, and let $M$ be a 1-periodic matching. If $\PER{G}$ admits a perfect matching, then either $M$ is perfect, or there exists an augmenting path of diameter $2d|E|$ starting from every free vertex.
: proof
  Let $M^{*}$ be a perfect matching for $\PER{G}$, not necessarily periodic. Consider the symmetric difference $M \oplus M^{*}$.
  This is a collection of vertex-disjoint cycles and paths (possibly infinite), where every path endpoint is a free vertex with respect to $M$, every free vertex is the endpoint of a path (because $M^{*}$ is perfect), and each path is alternating with respect to $M$. Let $\cal P$ be this collection of alternating paths.
  Consider the hypercube $H:=\{1,\ldots,K\}^d$, and define $\PER{V}_H$ to be the set of vertices $v^{\vec x}$ with $\vec x \in H$.
  By symmetry, if $M$ is not perfect, then there is at least one free vertex in $\PER{G}$, and thus there are $K^d$ copies of that free vertex in $\PER{V}_H$, and each is the endpoint of an alternating path in $\cal P$. Let $\cal P'\subseteq \cal P$ be the paths of $\cal P$ with an endpoint at a copy of that free vertex in $\PER{V}_H$.
  Suppose that none of the paths in $\cal P'$ are fully contained in $\PER{V}_H$.
  Because the paths are vertex-disjoint, all $K^d$ paths exit $\PER{V}_H$.
  The boundary of $H$ is connected to $2dK^{d-1}$ lattice points outside of $H$. Each path in $\cal P'$ must exit $\PER{V}_H$ into one of them. By the Pigeonhole Principle, one of them is entered by at least $\frac{K^d}{2dK^{d-1}} = K/2d$ edges. As $\PER{G}$ is local, we have
  $K/2d \leq |E|$, or $K \leq 2d|E|$.
  Therefore, if $K = 2d|E|+1$, then one of the paths in $\cal P'$ must be contained in $\PER{V}_H$, that is, its diameter is at most $2d|E|$.

We define a ***bipartite periodic graph*** $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$ to satisfy $V=R\cup B$ and every edge $(u^{\vec x},v^{\vec y}) \in \PER{E}$ has one protovertex (say, $u$) in $R$ and the other protovertex (say, $v$) in $B$.
That is, the 2-coloring of the bipartite graph is preserved by the periodicity.
By the following lemma, every connected periodic graph that is bipartite can be made bipartite periodic by doubling the period in each dimension.

: lemma
  If a periodic graph $\PER{G}$ is bipartite and connected, then its bipartition is 2-periodic.
: proof
  Consider the 2-coloring induced by the bipartition of $\PER{G}$, which is unique up to renaming of color classes, as the graph $\PER{G}$ is connected.
  For any vertex $v^{\vec x}$ and $\vec{\Delta}\in\mathbb Z^d$, consider a path $P$ between $v^{\vec x}$ and $v^{\vec x + \vec\Delta}$, and construct a path $P'$ from $v^{\vec x}$ to $v^{\vec x + 2\vec\Delta}$ by concatenating $P$ and $P+\vec\Delta$, its translation by $\vec\Delta$. By construction, $P'$ is of even length, and thus $v^{\vec x+2\vec\Delta}$ and $v^{\vec x}$ are in the same color class.

Note that this lemma does not hold if the graph is not connected. For example, take a 1D periodic graph with one protovertex $v$ and one protoedge $(v^{0}, v^{p})$ with $p$ prime, the periodicity of any 2-coloring of this graph is at least $2p$. Locality does not help either as each edge can be split into $p$ local edges. For instance, let $V=\{v_0,\ldots,v_{p-1}\}$ and $E=\{(v_i^{0},v_{i+1}^{1})\mid i=0,\ldots,p-2\}\cup\{(v_{p-1}^0,v_0^1)\}$, with $p$ prime. The local 1D periodic graph $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$ is bipartite, but the periodicity of any 2-coloring of this graph is again at least $2p$.


: lemma @=lem:bipartiteaugmenting
  Let $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$ be a $d$-dimensional bipartite periodic graph, and let $M$ be a 1-periodic matching.
  If $\PER{G}$ admits a perfect matching, then either $M$ is perfect, or there exists an augmenting path of length less than $|V|$, where each protovertex appears at most once.

: proof
  Because $E$ is finite, $\PER{G}$ has finite locality,
  so we can expand $\PER{G}$ into an equivalent local graph $\PER{G'}$.
  By Lemma~@lem:shortaugmenting, if $\PER{G'}$ admits a perfect matching and $M$ is imperfect, then there is a finite augmenting path, that is, a finite simple alternating walk joining two free vertices.
  This path exists in both $\PER{G'}$ and $\PER{G}$ because they represent the same infinite graph.
  Take a *shortest* alternating walk $P=(v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1},v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2},\ldots,v_{i_k}^{\vec x_k})$
  that joins two free vertices in $\PER{G}$
  but where we allow vertices and edges to repeat (so it may not be augmenting).
  Because finite alternating walks have odd length and $\PER{G}$ is bipartite periodic,
  one endpoint must be in $\PER{R}$ and the other endpoint must be in $\PER{B}$.
  Assume without loss of generality that $v_{i_1}\in B$ and $v_{i_k}\in R$.
  Suppose for contradiction that some protovertex appears more than once in $P$.
  Then we claim we can construct a shorter alternating walk, contradicting that $P$ was shortest.
  Let $j$ be the smallest integer for which protovertex $v_{i_j}$ appears more than once in $P$, and let $j' > j$ be the index of the second appearance $v_{i_{j'}}^{\vec x_{j'}}$ of the same protovertex $v_{i_j} = v_{i_{j'}}$. Then the walk $P$ and its translation $P+{\vec \Delta}$ by $\vec \Delta := \vec x_{j}-\vec x_{j'}$ intersect at $v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j} = v_{i_{j'}}^{\vec x_{j'}+\vec\Delta}$.
  Because $P$ starts and ends at free vertices, and $M$ is periodic so vertex freedom is preserved under translations by $\vec \Delta$, we must have $1 < j < k$.
  Thus vertex $v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j}$ must be incident to an edge in~$M$ that is both in $P$ and in $P+\vec \Delta$; it cannot be $(v_{i_{j-1}}^{\vec x_{j-1}}, v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j})$
  or else that edge would be in both $P$ and $P+\vec \Delta$, so $v_{i_{j-1}} = v_{i_{j'-1}}$, contradicting that $j$ is smallest.
  Hence, the edge $(v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j}, v_{i_{j+1}}^{\vec x_{j+1}})$ is in $M$, and is common to both $P$ and $P+\vec \Delta$.
  Because $\PER{G}$ is bipartite periodic, both $P$ and $P+\vec \Delta$ start at a vertex in $\PER{B}$, so this edge is oriented the same in both walks, and thus
  $(v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j}, v_{i_{j+1}}^{\vec x_{j+1}}) =
  (v_{i_{j'}}^{\vec x_{j'}+\vec \Delta}, v_{i_{j'+1}}^{\vec x_{j'+1}+\vec \Delta})$.
  We can now construct a shorter alternating walk $P'=(v_{i_1}^{\vec x_1},v_{i_2}^{\vec x_2},\ldots,v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j},v_{i_{j'+1}}^{\vec x_{j'+1}+\vec \Delta},\ldots,v_{i_k}^{\vec x_k+\vec \Delta})$, skipping $j'-j > 0$ vertices strictly between $v_{i_j}^{\vec x_j}$ and $v_{i_{j'+1}}^{\vec x_{j'+1}}$.
  Its endpoints are translates of the original free endpoints, so this walk also connects two free vertices with the same protovertices.
  This walk may repeat vertices and edges, which is why we needed to allow repetitions when defining $P$. But in the end, we show that the walk cannot repeat a protovertex, so it cannot actually repeat a vertex, and thus it is a valid augmenting path.
  It contains at most $|V|$ vertices, so its length is less than~$|V|$.

: theorem @=thm:periodicmatching
  If a $d$-dimensional bipartite periodic graph $\PER{G}$ admits a perfect matching, then it admits a 1-periodic perfect matching.

: proof
  %First make $\PER{G}$ local, which blows up the graph size by a factor of $k^d$ if the locality is initially~$k$.
  Suppose $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$ admits a perfect matching $M^{*}$.
  We build a periodic perfect matching $\PER{M}$ incrementally by finding augmenting paths using Lemma~{@lem:bipartiteaugmenting}.
  As we maintain that the matching $\PER{M}$ is 1-periodic, it suffices to keep track of the set $M$ of protoedges in the matching, and initially $M=\emptyset$. A protovertex is ***free*** if none of its vertices is incident to an edge in $\PER{M}$.
  We can reduce the number of free protovertices to zero as follows:
  1. Take an augmenting path $P$ without any repeating protovertices, which is known to exist by Lemma~{@lem:bipartiteaugmenting}.
  2. Take all translations of $P$ by vectors in $\mathbb Z^d$.
  3. As each protovertex appears only once in $P$, all translations of $P$ are disjoint. Then augment $\PER{M}$ by the union of all translations of $P$, thereby reducing the number of free vertices. This can be done in linear time by just updating $M$.
  4. Repeat until no free protovertices remain. \qedhere

Here again, the validity of this theorem relies on the periodic graph being bipartite periodic. Being bipartite alone would not suffice, as can be seen by taking the same example graph as earlier with one protovertex $v$ and one protoedge $(v^{0}, v^{p})$ with $p$ prime. This graph admits a perfect matching but its periodicity is at least $2p$. The same holds for the local 1D periodic graph shown earlier.

: theorem @=thm:matchingalgorithm
  Given a $d$-dimensional bipartite periodic graph $\PER{G}=(\PER{V},\PER{E})$, we can compute a perfect matching for $\PER{G}$, or determine that none exists, in
  $O(|E| \sqrt{|V|})$. The returned perfect matching is 1-periodic.
  
: proof 
  Given $V$ and $E$, we construct a graph $G'=(V,E')$ mapping the periodic graph onto a $d$-dimensional torus. For this, project each protoedge $(u^{\vec x},v^{\vec y})\in E$ onto an edge $(u,v)\in E'$. Duplicates can be removed. 
  If $\PER{G}$ admits a 1-periodic perfect matching $M$, then its projection $M'$ is a perfect matching in $G'$. Conversely, any perfect matching $M'$ in $G'$ can be lifted to a 1-periodic perfect matching $M$ in $\PER{G}$, by picking for each edge in $M'$, any edge that projects to it (and all its translations).
  Now we can use any finite perfect matching algorithm on $G'$, such as the $O(|E| \sqrt{|V|})$ Hopcroft–Karp algorithm \cite{hopcroft1973n}.
%Just as for the previous problems, the Periodic Perfect Matching problem is in co-RE by a standard compactness argument.

# Tiling Hardness

We model (discrete) tiling of $d$-dimensional (sub)spaces by polycubes
as tiling in the integer lattice~$\mathbb Z^d$.
Specifically, a ***prototile*** $P$ is a finite subset $P\subset \mathbb Z^d$,
and $P$ ***tiles*** a subset $E\subseteq \mathbb Z^d$ ***by translations*** $A\subseteq \mathbb Z^d$ if
1. the set $E$ is covered without overlap, that is, for every $e\in E$, there is exactly one translation $a\in A$ and one $p\in P$ such that $e=a+p$; and
2. for every translation $a\in A$, the translated tile is within $E$, that is, $a+P\subseteq E$.
More generally, for tiling with multiple prototiles $P_1,\ldots,P_k$,
we specify a set $A_i \subseteq \mathbb Z^d$ of translations
for each prototile~$P_i$,
and the translated tiles must jointly cover $E$ without overlap.
Unless we explicitly say "tiling by translation",
when we talk about tiling by one or more prototiles,
we implicitly add all rotations (and optionally reflections)
of the prototiles to the prototile set.

In ***$d$D periodic subspace tiling***,
the target subset $E\subseteq\mathbb Z^d$ is
required to be ***periodic*** meaning that it is invariant under
$d$ independent translations, that is, there are $d$ linearly independent
vectors $\vec v_1,\ldots,\vec v_d\in\mathbb Z^d$ such that
$E + \vec v_i = E$ for all $i=1,\ldots,d$.
In $(d+\frac12)$D, the ambient space is $\mathbb Z^d \times \{1,\ldots,W\}$,
but $E$ is still required to have only $d$ translation vectors.
Although periodic subsets are infinite, they can be described finitely as
a subset $E'$ of a finite fundamental domain $D$ that tiles the ambient space
under the $d$ given translation vectors;
this is the input to the periodic subspace tiling problem.
***Periodic tiling completion*** is the special case where the
finite target pattern $E'$ consists of the points in $D$ not covered by
a given non-overlapping periodic placement of tiles, specified up to the
same translations.
We start with a general membership result:

: lemma @=lem:15d-tiling-pspace
  Periodic subspace tiling, and hence periodic tiling completion,
  is in co-RE when all prototiles have polynomial-size bounding boxes.[^1]
  [^1]:
    For connected prototiles, this condition
    is equivalent to having polynomial size.
  In 1.5D, the same problems are in PSPACE.

: proof
  We reduce periodic subspace tiling to Periodic CNF SAT,
  which is in co-RE by Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat.
  Suppose we are given a finite fundamental domain $D$,
  a subset $E'\subseteq D$,
  and $d$ translation vectors $\vec v_1,\ldots,\vec v_d$
  defining the periodic target subset
  $E=\{e+\tau(\vec z) \mid e\in E',\ \vec z\in\mathbb Z^d\}$,
  where $\tau(\vec z) = \sum_{j=1}^d z_j\vec v_j$.
  Any point $x$ in the ambient space can be
  written $x=a_x+\tau(\vec z_x)$ for a unique $a_x\in D$.

  For each prototile $P_i$,
  and for each translation representative $a\in D$,
  introduce a protovariable $X_{i,a}$.
  Variable $X_{i,a}^{\vec z}$ represents whether a placed copy of $P_i$
  is translated by $a + \tau(\vec z)$.
  We add the following finite set of protoclauses, each of which represents all of its periodic translates.
  For every target point $q\in E'$, add a protoclause saying that at least one tile placement covers $q$: $\bigvee_{i,p\in P_i} X_{i,a_{q-p}}^{\vec z_{q-p}}$.
  For every pair of distinct overlapping placements, add a protoclause forbidding both placements: $\neg X_{i,a}^{\vec 0} \lor \neg X_{i',a'}^{\vec r}$ whenever $(a+P_i)\cap(a'+\tau(\vec r)+P_{i'})\neq\emptyset$.
  Here $\vec r$ is polynomially bounded because
  every prototile has a polynomial-size bounding box,
  and two placements can overlap only if their bounding boxes overlap.
  Thus, for fixed dimension, there are only polynomially many such overlap protoclauses.
  Finally,
  for every placement that would cover a point outside the target subset,
  add a protoclause forbidding that placement:
  $\neg X_{i,a}^{\vec 0}$ whenever $a+P_i\not\subseteq E$.
  The satisfying assignments are exactly the tilings of the target subset~$E$.
  The construction has polynomial size (in fixed dimension)
  and polynomial locality
  because every prototile has a polynomial-size bounding box.

  In 1.5D, the same construction gives a 1D Periodic CNF SAT instance
  (with the bounded coordinate encoded in the protovariables),
  so PSPACE membership follows from Theorem~@thm:periodic-cnf-sat.

  Periodic tiling completion reduces to subspace tiling as follows.
  Reduce every point of every preplaced tile modulo the given translations
  to its representative in~$D$.
  If any representative occurs twice, then the periodic placement overlaps;
  otherwise, take $E'$ to be the points of $D$ whose representatives do not occur.

## Tromino + Periodic @=sec:subspace
% https://coauthor.csail.mit.edu/6.890/m/tbFNJMup5k5WDPCqy
% https://cocreate.csail.mit.edu/r/qJhBEstF7aYvwbhjM
% https://coauthor.csail.mit.edu/6.890/m/tbFNJMup5k5WDPCqy

%We now turn to the problem of tiling the plane with polyominoes. 
We begin by proving hardness of subspace tiling (Problem~@prob:subspace)
with trominoes, which has a relatively short proof given the reduction
infrastructure we built in Section~@sec:periodic-reductions.
Later (Theorem~@thm:periodic-completion) we will strengthen this result to
tiling completion, but the proof is more complicated.

A ***tromino*** is a (connected) polyomino of size 3, i.e., a connected size-3 subset of $\mathbb Z^2$.
There are two trominoes up to rotations: the \L (L) tromino and the \I (I) tromino.

: theorem @=thm:periodicsubset
  Tiling a given periodic subset $E\subseteq \mathbb Z^2$ with copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable. In 1.5D, the problem is PSPACE-complete.

: proof
  Membership follows from Lemma~@lem:15d-tiling-pspace.
  Hardness is by reduction from local Periodic Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation from Theorem~@thm:planar-graph-orientation.
  Given a local periodic planar drawing of the graph instance, construct a local planar orthogonal periodic drawing of polynomial grid size, using Theorem~@thm:orthodraw.
  Apply Lemma~@lem:degree3 to further refine so that all degree-3 vertices connect locally left, up, and right;
  and all trichromatic vertices appear in the orientation blue-red-green or green-red-blue (rotating via Figure~@fig:rotation).
  %all other orientations can be rotated into that one using the rotation gadget

  Let $M$ be the grid size of the resulting periodic drawing.
  Overlay the $M\times M$ grid graph with our drawing and consider the dual grid graph whose faces are $1/M \times 1/M$ squares. Each square intersects the drawing in a few possible ways:
  1. ***Wire***: a horizontal or vertical edge portion (red, green or blue).
  2. ***Wire bend***: an edge bend (red, green or blue) in one of 4 orientations.
  3. ***Monochromatic vertex***: a monochromatic 1-in-3 degree-3 vertex (red, green or blue), in one orientation (left, up, right).
  4. ***Trichromatic vertex***: a trichromatic 0-or-3-in-3 vertex, of degree 3, in one of two orientations (blue-red-green or green-red-blue).
  5. The square face does not intersect the drawing.

  We transform the graph drawing into a periodic set $E\subseteq \mathbb Z^2$ with translation vectors $(0,6M)$ and $(6M,0)$. We only need to specify the elements in $E$ within the $[1,6M]^2$ square. Subdivide that square into $M\times M$ subsquares, each $6 \times 6$ corresponding to one of the square faces of the dual graph above, and within each of these subsquares, include elements in $E$ depending on the intersection type, as specified in Figure~@fig:Lgadgets for \L trominoes and Figure~@fig:Igadgets for \I trominoes, where $E$ consists of all ***pixels*** that are not dark gray.
  Figure~@fig:reductionexample gives complete examples of the reductions.

  Figures~@fig:Lgadgets and~@fig:Igadgets show the only possible tilings for each gadget:
  1. Wire and wire bend: There are only two ways to tile the gadget. The edge orientation is encoded by the vector from the (\I or \L) tromino center to the pixel matching the color of the edge.
  2. Monochromatic vertex: There are three solutions, determined by the edge covering the center pixel.
  3. Trichromatic vertex: There are two solutions, and the presence of a tromino in the center of the gadget determines if all edges are oriented inwards or outwards.

  Thus, the periodic subset $E$ can be tiled by trominoes if and only if the local Periodic Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation instance has a solution.

