: corollary @=cor:periodicsubsettranslation
  Tiling a given periodic subset $E\subseteq \mathbb Z^2$ with translations of two polyominoes (the I trominoes \I and \Ir) is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable. In 1.5D, the problem is PSPACE-complete.

The same techniques give a simpler proof of NP-hardness for the finite case,
as previously shown for \I by
[[Beauquier-Nivat-Remila-Robson-1995,Horiyama-Ito-Nakatsuka-Suzuki-Uehara-2017]]
and for \L by [[Moore-Robson-2001,Horiyama-Ito-Nakatsuka-Suzuki-Uehara-2017]].
Indeed, this proof can be seen as a direct simplification of
[[Horiyama-Ito-Nakatsuka-Suzuki-Uehara-2017]].

: corollary @=cor:finite-periodicsubset
  Tiling a given finite subset $E\subseteq \mathbb Z^2$
  with copies of a single tromino (\L or \I) is NP-complete.

: proof
  Apply the same reduction as in Theorem~@thm:periodicsubset, but starting from finite Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation of Theorem~@thm:planar-graph-orientation.
  The drawing step becomes simpler: a polynomial-grid orthogonal drawing follows from [[Biedl-Kant-1998]] (or by applying the finite analog of Theorem~@thm:orthodraw).
  Then, as before, apply the local refinement of Lemma~@lem:degree3
  and substitute the same $6\times 6$ gadgets within the bounding box of the finite drawing.
  The result is a finite subset $E$ that is tilable if and only if the graph-orientation instance has a solution.
  Membership in NP follows because a tiling of finite $E$ consists of $|E|/3$ trominoes.

## $O(1)$-omino + Disconnected Polyomino @=sec:two-polyomino

Next we show how to simulate the periodic target subset of $\mathbb Z^2$
with a single (disconnected) polyomino, while blowing up the tromino
by a constant factor to prevent it from tiling the plane on its own.

: theorem @=thm:twopolyomino
  Tiling with two polyominoes, one of which is connected and of constant size, and the other disconnected with polynomial-size bounding box, is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable. In 1.5D, the problem is PSPACE-complete.

: proof
  Membership follows from Lemma~@lem:15d-tiling-pspace.
  For hardness, we proceed as in the previous section by reducing from local Periodic Planar Trichromatic Graph Orientation and transforming the instance into a periodic set $E$ with translation vectors $(6M,0)$ and $(0,6M)$. Let $E_0 := E \cap [1,6M]^2$. This is the pattern that is repeated by translation to produce the periodic set to be tiled.
  Now consider the complement $\bar E_0 :=[1,6M]^2 \setminus E_0$. If the graph orientation problem has a solution, then $E_0$ and the \I tromino tile the plane as shown in the previous section. However, the \I tromino can tile the plane on its own, so we need to modify both tiles so that neither tiles the plane on its own, and so that the pattern $E_0$ combines with copies of itself in a unique way to produce the complement of $E$.

  To prevent the first (connected) polyomino from tiling the plane on its own, we refine the integer lattice, replacing every pixel by a $3\times 3$ square of 9 pixels. The \I tromino is replaced with the shape shown in Figure~@fig:bumpytromino which is produced by replacing each of the three tromino pixels by 5 sub-pixels forming a $+$ pattern, producing a 15-omino $P$.
  Likewise, we refine $E_0$ into $E_0' \subseteq [1,18M]^2$ by replacing each pixel by the same pattern, and $E'$ by translating $E'_0$ with vectors $(18M,0)$ and $(0,18M)$. See Figure~@fig:0-or-3-plus for the substitution applied to the trichromatic gadget.
  Any tiling of the refinement is strictly equivalent to a tiling of the original set $E$ with the \I tromino.

  : figure
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.8]{figs/polyominoes/I+}
    \caption: The 15-omino $P$ obtained by refining the \I tromino.
    @=fig:bumpytromino

  : figure
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figs/I/0-or-3-in-3/gadgetGRB-plus}
    \hfill
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figs/I/0-or-3-in-3/gadgetGRB-sol0-plus}
    \hfill
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figs/I/0-or-3-in-3/gadgetGRB-sol3-plus}
    \caption: Refinement of the trichromatic gadget and its two solutions.
    @=fig:0-or-3-plus

  To see that $P$ does not tile the plane, fix a copy of $P$ and orient it horizontally as in Figure~@fig:bumpytromino.
  Consider the pocket of two adjacent uncovered pixels immediately above the long bar between two consecutive bumps.
  To avoid overlapping this copy, any copy of $P$ covering either pocket pixel must do so with the tip of a $+$, leaving the other pocket pixel completely surrounded and thus uncoverable.

  By construction of the orthogonal drawing, the $6\times 6$ squares at the four corners of $[1,6M]^2$ do not intersect the drawing and therefore do not intersect $E$, so these squares are totally filled in $\bar E_0$. In the refinement $\bar E'_0$, the corresponding $18\times 18$ squares are totally filled as well. We construct the second, disconnected polyomino $Q$ by taking $\bar E'_0$ and replacing the $18\times 18$ squares at the corners by the key/lock patterns shown in Figure~@fig:keycorners. These patterns are designed with complementary parts that can only match each other so that any tiling with $Q$ must glue other $Q$'s in a grid pattern.

  : figure
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.4]{figs/polyominoes/keycorners}
    \caption: The four corner key/lock patterns used to construct polyomino $Q$.
    @=fig:keycorners

  Because $P$ does not tile on its own, there must be at least one copy of $Q$ in the tiling, and every such copy must combine with other copies of $Q$ to form a grid pattern. Together, they form exactly the complement of $E'$, which in turn can be tiled with $P$ if and only if the graph orientation problem has a solution.

The previous proof uses only translations of $Q$. Also, $P$ has 2-fold rotational symmetry, so all its copies in the tiling are translations of two polyominoes (without rotation). Thus we obtain the following corollary:

: corollary @=cor:threepolyomino-translation
  Tiling with three polyominoes by translation, two of which are connected and of constant size, and the third disconnected with polynomial-size bounding box, is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable. In 1.5D, the problem is PSPACE-complete.

By standard universal Turing machine arguments, we obtain hardness
for tiling completion with a fixed set of (constant-size) prototiles:

: corollary @=cor:twopolyomino-fixed-completion
  Tiling completion is co-RE-complete with two fixed polyominoes,
  starting from a finite preplacement.

Prototile $Q$ would be connected if its complement (the scaled shape
being tiled by trominoes) had holes only in the four corners of each cell.
Unfortunately, interior holes seem necessary, at least for \I trominoes:
tiling a finite hole-free polyomino with a given rectangular prototile
can be solved in polynomial time [[Kenyon-Kenyon-1992]].

## Two Connected Polycubes

With one additional layer, we can make the disconnected polyomino connected:

: corollary @=cor:twopolycube
  Tiling 3D with two connected polycubes, one of which is of constant size,
  is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable.
  The same result holds for tiling a height-$Z$ 2.5D slab for every fixed $Z>1$.

: proof
  We start with the height-$Z$ 2.5D slab, for a fixed $Z > 1$.
  Call the finite $Z$ direction "vertical" and the two infinite directions "horizontal".
  Let $P,Q$ be the polyominoes in the previous proof, and let $B=[1,18M]^2$.
  By increasing $M$ if necessary, assume that $18M > Z$.
  Define the two polycubes as follows:
  $\hat P := P\times\{1,2,\ldots,Z-1\}$ extrudes $P$ vertically by height $Z-1$,
  and $\hat Q := (Q\times\{1,2,\ldots,Z-1\})\cup(B\times\{Z\})$
  extrudes $Q$ vertically by height $Z-1$ and adds a full $18M\times18M$ layer on top.
  Both polycubes $\hat P$ and $\hat Q$ are connected.
  In its horizontal orientation, $\hat P$ has dimensions $9\times3\times(Z-1)$,
  so every nonhorizontal orientation has vertical span $3$ or~$9$.

  Next we argue that tilings of the slab with $\hat P$ and $\hat Q$ that use at least one copy of $\hat Q$ are equivalent to 2D tilings with $P$ and $Q$.
  Because $\hat Q$ contains the full $18M\times18M$ layer $B\times\{Z\}$,
  it cannot be turned perpendicular to the slab.
  The key/lock patterns of $Q$ force the copies of $\hat Q$ into the same grid pattern.
  Neighboring copies of $\hat Q$ cannot have opposite vertical orientations: in one outer layer, a key protrusion of one copy would meet the full layer of the other copy, causing overlap.
  Thus all copies of $\hat Q$ have the same vertical orientation, up to reflecting the entire tiling vertically.
  The remaining space is $Z-1$ identical layers of the original periodic set $E'$.
  When $Z=2$, no copy of $\hat P$ can be turned perpendicular to the slab.
  When $Z>2$, the refinement $E'$ contains no $2\times2$ square, whereas every nonhorizontal orientation of $\hat P$ has a horizontal cross-section containing a $2\times2$ square.
  Thus every copy of $\hat P$ in the remaining space is oriented horizontally, with its extrusion vertical, so this space is tiled by $\hat P$ exactly when $E'$ is tiled by $P$.

  It remains to check that $\hat P$ cannot tile the slab by itself.
  For $Z=2$, every copy must lie parallel to the slab, so this follows from the planar pocket argument in the proof of Theorem~@thm:twopolyomino.
  For $Z=3$, a nonhorizontal copy spans all three layers and leaves a slot of height~$1$ between two consecutive bumps on a boundary of the slab.
  Any copy of $\hat P$ covering a voxel in this slot also covers the voxel immediately inward, which is already covered by the long bar.
  Thus no nonhorizontal copy of $\hat P$ can occur, and the planar pocket argument applies again.
  For $Z\geq4$, the extrusion has thickness at least~$3$.
  Fix a copy of $\hat P$ and consider the two pocket voxels in an interior layer of its extrusion.
  Any copy of $\hat P$ covering one of these voxels must be horizontal,
  or else it would overlap either the long bar or a bounding bump;
  thus the planar pocket argument leaves the other voxel uncoverable.

  For full 3D, define $\hat P := P\times\{2,3,4\}$ and start with
  $\hat Q := (B\times\{1,5\})\cup(Q\times\{2,3,4\})$,
  extruding $P$ and $Q$ through three simulation layers,
  and sandwiching the extrusion of $Q$ between two full $18M\times18M$ layers.
  Carve locks into the bottom full layer of $\hat Q$ and add matching keys protruding into layer~$6$ above its top full layer.
  These complementary keys force copies of $\hat Q$ into successive horizontal grids with vertical displacement~$5$, while the two full layers keep the keys disjoint from the simulation layers.
  The thickness-$3$ pocket argument from the 2.5D case with $Z \geq 4$ prevents $\hat P$ from tiling 3D by itself, so the same equivalence applies.
  %Membership in co-RE is standard by compactness.

: corollary @=cor:threepolycube-translation
  Tiling 2.5D or 3D with three connected polycubes by translation, two of which are of constant size, is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable. 

## Tromino Completion @=sec:completion

Next we strengthen Theorem~@thm:periodicsubset to tiling completion,
which requires a more complicated reduction:

: theorem @=thm:periodic-completion
  Given an infinite periodic partial tiling of the plane with one type of tromino (\L or \I), deciding whether it can be completed to a full tiling is co-RE-complete and thus undecidable. In 1.5D, the problem is PSPACE-complete.
: proof
  Membership follows from Lemma~@lem:15d-tiling-pspace.
  For hardness, this time we reduce from local Periodic Planar 3SAT-3 of Theorem~@thm:planar-3sat.
  As in the previous section, we start with the local planar graph representation of the SAT instance, and transform it into a local planar orthogonal periodic drawing of polynomial grid size, using Theorem~@thm:orthodraw, and further refine it so all degree-3 vertices connect locally left, up, and right, by Lemma~@lem:degree3.
  Rotate the graph by 45 degrees counterclockwise, and overlay the rotated square grid with a ***brick*** pattern, as shown in Figure~@fig:completionexample{(b)}. The pattern is built by bisecting horizontal rows of the rotated grid with horizontal lines, and within each row, bisecting successive vertices of the grid with vertical segments.
  To complete the reduction, we need to show how to implement 
  1. straight wires (2 directions),
  2. wire bends (all 4 rotations),
  3. 3SAT gadgets (1 orientation only),
  4. variable gadgets, and
  5. full bricks (to fill grid vertices not touched by edges of the graph),
  each by partially prefilling a brick with trominoes.
  Each brick has $\leq 4$ ***connectors***, two on its top side and two on its bottom side, each representing a boolean value. 

  In order to preserve parity in the gadgets, we represent a connector by a pair of pixels $p$ and $q$ (left-to-right) on the brick boundary, where one pixel is covered by a tromino from above, and the other from below. The value is true if $p$ is covered from below (meaning that all other pixels of the tile containing pixel $p$ are contained in the brick below $p$), and false if $q$ is covered from below.

  We decompose each brick into 5 ***subbricks***: A full-width ***major subbrick*** $K$ with $\leq 4$ connectors again, and four half-width ***minor subbricks*** $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$, each with one connector on the top and one on the bottom. To form a brick, we place subbricks $A$ and $B$ on top of $K$, and $C$ and $D$ at the bottom of $K$.

  The $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ subbricks implement one of four gadgets; see Figure~@fig:Lminor for \L trominoes and Figure~@fig:Iminor for \I trominoes:
  1. An ***equal*** gadget, ensuring the value of its top connector equals the value of its bottom connector.
  2. A ***not*** gadget ensuring the value of its top connector is the negation of the value of its bottom connector.
  3. A ***plug*** gadget, with only one connector (top or bottom) of any value.
  4. A ***filler*** gadget with no connector.
