    \begin{tikzpicture}
      % Lattice and brick parameters
      \def\cliprange{5}
      \def\drawrange{7}
      \def\brickw{1.4142}  % = sqrt(2)
      \def\brickh{0.7071}  % = sqrt(2)/2
      % Clip everything to a square
      \clip (-\cliprange,-\cliprange) rectangle (\cliprange,\cliprange);
      % --- Draw black rotated square lattice ---
      \begin{scope}[rotate=45]
        \foreach \x in {-7,...,7} {
          \draw[black] (\x,-\drawrange) -- (\x,\drawrange);
        }
        \foreach \y in {-7,...,7} {
          \draw[black] (-\drawrange,\y) -- (\drawrange,\y);
        }
      \end{scope}
      % --- Draw red brick pattern (unrotated) ---
      \foreach \i in {-10,...,10} {
        % Horizontal brick line between rows
        \pgfmathsetmacro{\y}{(\i + 0.5) * \brickh}
        \draw[red] (-10,\y) -- (10,\y);
        % Determine horizontal offset (aligned with row above)
        \ifodd\i
          \def\xshift{0.5}
        \else
          \def\xshift{0.0}
        \fi
        % Draw vertical segments aligned with row above
        \foreach \j in {-10,...,10} {
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\x}{(\j + \xshift) * \brickw}
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\ytop}{(\i + 1 + 0.5) * \brickh}  % next brick line
          \pgfmathsetmacro{\ybot}{(\i + 0.5) * \brickh}      % current brick line
          \draw[red] (\x,\ybot) -- (\x,\ytop);
        }
      }
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \caption: A rotated (black) square lattice with a (red) brick pattern.
    @=fig:brickpattern

: corollary @=cor:aperiodic-completion
  There exists a partial periodic covering of the plane by \L trominoes (respectively \I trominoes) that can be completed to a full tiling of the plane by \L trominoes (respectively \I trominoes), but all such completions are aperiodic.

Our result starts from an infinite seed of preplaced tiles.
This is necessary: tiling completion from a finite seed of trominoes
is decidable.

: theorem @=thm:finite-completion
  For either the \L or \I tromino,
  it is decidable whether a finite set of preplaced trominoes can be completed
  to a plane tiling with those trominoes.
  The problem is NP-complete when the preplacement has a polynomial-size bounding box.

: proof
  NP-hardness follows from our reductions in Theorem~@thm:periodic-completion, but starting from 3SAT instead of Periodic 3SAT. Then the construction fits in a polynomial-size rectangle, and the exterior is always tilable, so the problem reduces to whether the interior can be tiled, which is NP-hard.

  To prove the upper bounds, suppose a tiling exists.
  Let $B$ be the bounding box of the preplaced trominoes.
  The witness is the set of additional tiles that intersect $B$.
  This witness has polynomial size whenever $B$ does.
  Together with the preplaced trominoes, verify that this tiles all of $B$ and that all tiles intersect~$B$.
  We claim this is a witness, i.e., it implies the existence of a plane tiling completion.

  First, for \L trominoes:
  1. We can grow an edge of $B$ by $1$ by proceeding from one end to the other.
  2. If the next gap is $>1$, put an \L so that you fill two pixels, and one pixel in the next layer. Leave a gap that's 2 smaller.
  3. If the next gap is exactly $1$, put an \L so that you fill that one pixel, and two pixels in the next layer, pointing in the direction where you haven't filled anything yet so no collisions.
  4. Repeat on all sides to tile the plane.

  Second, for \I trominoes:
  1. Extend each column by stacking vertical {\I}s.
  2. Extend each row by stacking horizontal {\I}s.
  3. This leaves quarter-planes; pack those with horizontal {\I}s, say.
     \qedhere

The NP membership proof above is specific to the two trominoes:
it establishes the property that
covering the bounding box of preplaced tiles
guarantees that the remainder can always be tiled.
This property fails already for the L-shaped tetromino;
see Figure~@fig:tetromino-corner.
An intriguing open problem is whether finite-preplacement tiling completion
is decidable for a fixed (constant-size) single polyomino~$P$.
The best undecidability results for finite-preplacement tiling completion
for a fixed set of (constant-size) prototiles require
%36 polyominoes [[Robinson1971,Golomb1970]]
3 connected polygons [[Demaine-Langerman-2025]],
3 connected polyominoes
[[Stade2025-2tiling,kim2025undecidabilitytilingplaneset]],
or 1 connected and 1 disconnected polyomino
(Corollary~@cor:twopolyomino-fixed-completion).

: figure[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[scale=1.2]{figs/finite-completion/tetromino-corner}
  \caption:
    In this example, the tetrominoes cover the bounding box (dotted)
    of the preplaced green tetromino,
    but the remainder cannot be tiled by tetrominoes,
    because of the isolated cell marked with a red $\times$.
  @=fig:tetromino-corner

## Domino Tiling and Completion

Finally, we describe our positive results for tiling by dominoes \D,
which follow from our bipartite perfect matching results
in Section~@sec:matching.
The relationship between domino tilings and perfect matchings was previously established in [[Beauquier-Nivat-Remila-Robson-1995]].

: theorem @=thm:domino-periodic
  If a periodic polycube subset of $\mathbb R^d$ can be tiled by dominoes,
  then it can be tiled by dominoes periodically
  with at most double the original period.
: proof
  Consider the dual graph of the hypercubic lattice, and the subgraph $\PER{G}$ induced by the cells in the periodic subset to be tiled.
  The dual of the lattice, and thus $\PER{G}$, is bipartite with bipartition given by the parity of the sum of a cell's coordinates.
  Double each translation vector that does not preserve this parity,
  so that the representation of $\PER{G}$ is bipartite periodic.
  Any valid domino placement corresponds to an edge of $\PER{G}$; a tiling of the periodic subset corresponds to a perfect matching; and any perfect matching corresponds to a tiling of the periodic subset.
  By Theorem~@thm:periodicmatching, if $\PER{G}$ has a perfect matching, then it has one that is periodic with respect to the possibly doubled translation vectors.

: corollary @=cor:domino-completion-periodic
  Any periodic partial tiling of $\mathbb R^d$ by dominoes that can be completed
  can be completed periodically with at most double the original period.

Applying Theorem~@thm:matchingalgorithm, we obtain the following.

: corollary @=cor:domino-periodic-polytime
  Tiling a periodic polycube subset of $d$D with dominoes can be decided in polynomial time, in any dimension $d$.


